e undead can never die but every  death brings you closer to hollowing and  once you've hollowed completely you lose  all hope so the reason I said a similar  thing twice is because the gameplay just  mirrored and experienced this is the  story of an undead who gives up hope  it's the experience of a player who  fights against dark souls this is a game  that has depth because it focuses on the  experience and Dark Souls is known as a  difficult game it's the story of an  undead human in a world where everything  is out to kill him  a world where dying as this undead is a  traumatizing experience that you have to  try and push through just like dying as  a player is an irritating experience  that you have to overcome so it makes  sense you know that you should have  trouble progressing in this game  sometimes what a lot of people don't  understand is that Dark Souls isn't  difficult for the sake of being  difficult its difficulty comes as a  result of a deeper design philosophy  from software have actively designed  their game around something that only  games have a failure state death in  video games is something we take for  granted at this point but in Dark Souls  death and failure are designed to give  you that feeling of struggle and triumph  it's also used to immerse yourself in a  world where death doesn't end your life  but it brings you closer and closer to  giving up people often say it's too  difficult or it doesn't explain things  to you  these are insults leveled at the Soul  series regularly and they're right Dark  Souls is too difficult for some people  Dark Souls also doesn't go out of its  way to explain things to you but here's  something you have to understand about  Dark Souls it's a game that doesn't dumb  down the experience for the benefit of  the so called mass-market appeal it does  its own thing regardless of whether some  people will be turned off and in doing  so it's become something infinitely more  valuable and pure Fromm's philosophy  revolves around making this good game  first and trusting that people will come  and this has happened slowly you know  Demon Souls came out and it was a niche  hit Dark Souls came out and even 2 years  after the game came out people are still  picking it up due to word-of-mouth as a  result the soul series doesn't need huge  marketing efforts behind it because it's  become this industry icon through  word-of-mouth and people telling each  other they should play it you know  someone said something really great to  me recently he said you know from  software they aren't afraid to put  things in their game that people will  miss  and that's such a perfectly beautiful  statement if I had to choose any  statement that represented Dark Souls  I'd probably choose this one it's the  reason the game has so much depth  because they don't compromise on game  design just so every player can see an  area or so that every player can  understand and play their game as soon  as they start when I say that you might  not see certain things I'm not just  talking about how the dlc is located in  a hard-to-see cave near an optional boss  that can only be accessed if you kill  certain enemies in the right order no  I'm also talking about how they don't  shove the story down your throat or how  the game locks you out of certain areas  if you're not good enough to beat them  let me explain that better imagine if  you were reading a book you're reading a  book and then all of a sudden the book  shuts itself and says stop you're not  good enough to read me I'm going to lock  away parts so you have to earn them Dark  Souls sets itself apart from other games  in this way and this is just one of the  reasons it's come to be considered a  pure gaming experience it's something I  think that could help games remember  what they can be I think games should  generally focus on what makes them  different from other forms of media and  that's something that from software has  captured in a lot of different ways but  Dark Souls doesn't just lock you out of  content by being difficult in fact I  think one of the biggest reasons Dark  Souls is considered difficult is not  because it's hard but because it's hard  to get into and while I see this as a  bit of a problem since they plan to fix  this in Dark Souls 2 and make the game  more accessible some fans will even  argue that the inaccessibility of Dark  Souls is a plus because it makes those  first few hours of torment and struggle  even more rewarding when you finally  learn how to play but it's not just the  gameplay in Dark Souls that's cryptic  and anyone who's on my channel knows  this Dark Souls also approached the  concept of story in a game in a unique  way the story elements are cryptic and  hidden down to the last little detail  and while you might have some idea of  what happened by the end of the game  most players actually just end up  thinking why the [ __ ] am I on fire  there's actually a beautiful little  story behind why the souls games were  created with these cryptic hidden  stories I'm sure many of you have  realized this but Dark Souls is a West  installed RPG created in Japan and  that's a bit strange  it even has English voice actors to  convey this feeling more accurately so  why is this why is this a vision  well when miyazaki the game's director  was a child he loved reading western  fantasy  he loved the stories the settings and he  was inspired and so Dark Souls was  created in this Western RPG format but  there's more to this story Miyazaki  couldn't read English very well so when  he read these Western fantasy novels he  had gaps and you might be able to see  where I'm going with this some parts of  these books he just couldn't read and so  while he had this vision of decaying  castles and brave knights he had to use  his imagination to fill the gaps and in  doing so he created a story that was  partly his own and a story that was  richer because of this fact sound  familiar  when we play Dark Souls we create our  own stories just like Miyazaki did we  read item descriptions we listen to  dialogue and from the bones and  fragments of a story we slowly carefully  craft this vision of a world some people  like me bring this vision to life  through videos we call this vision law  some of us share our law with others and  discover other people's stories on image  boards and forums inspiring and  challenging great ideas and this is what  has kept this incredible community alive  way longer than you'd expect by creating  a game that intentionally leaves things  for you to discover and speculate on we  now have one of the strongest  communities on YouTube and we exist  across hundreds of pages of forums and  wikis there's a reason people were still  coming into this game years after the  initial marketing push it's because we  in the Dark Souls community talked about  this game we share our experiences and  there are thousands of people who play  just because they get that comment from  a friend that says dude you have to play  this game or when someone says you  haven't played Dark Souls I can't  imagine not having a friend to talk to  about my experiences in this game you  know  the thrill you get when your world is  invaded by another player or the venting  of frustration that you have to do when  you've been stuck on a boss for four  hours and then the eventual ecstasy you  get when you overcome an obstacle that  was in your way it's just one of those  games that was destined to find a home  on the internet full of people who need  to share and tell others about this  experience and in this way the game  actually kind of sells itself I recently  got invited to the Namco Bandai offices  in San Jose in the States and I managed  to meet all these people who were behind  the marketing of Dark Souls and Dark  Souls 2 and the thing is they realized  that this game is different they  realized it has this effect on people  where word-of-mouth will sell their game  and really they just have to allow from  software to fulfill their vision and I  can't think of any better publisher to  publish this actually I met with Brian  Hong who's the director of marketing for  Dark Souls and Dark Souls 2 and he put  it so well they don't have to change  Dark Souls to suit the market just like  Apple didn't create a PC to suit the  market the market eventually realized  their vision and came around to them and  that's what I think is happening with  Dark Souls and I think this is a game  that will come to define a generation  and I'm actually honored to be a part of  it with you guys soon you'll be able to  click here and Kindle the bonfire so  that we can make this channel better  together in the comments of this video  I'd love for you to tell me your  favorite little piece of gameplay design  and Dark Souls I could have made this  video much longer if I talked about all  my favorite mechanics and bits but I  decided to keep it short but sweet for  example though I could quote so many  beautiful pieces of gameplay design from  the design works book and these have  interviews with the director and the  developers so I think I might just read  one of my favorite quotes right now so  one of them is about his design of the  undead the undead dragon and he says and  Miyazaki says most people don't believe  me when I say this but a certain kind of  refinement elegance and dignity are very  important to me I usually tell the  designers that flat-out grotesque or  splatter type designs will not get past  me this has everything to do with my own  personal sensibilities and it's  something that I apply to every design  that I  it's a little hard to describe if you  were to ask me well what do you mean by  dignity I guess the best answer I can  provide you is right here in this book  in every design in Dark Souls you see  within these pages my idea of dignity is  something that I pursue with every  project I work on and then one of the  other designers tells the story he says  I remember when I was drawing the undead  dragon I submitted a design draft that  depicted a dragon swarming with maggots  and other gross things Miyazaki handed  this back to me saying this isn't  dignified don't rely on the gross factor  to portray an undead dragon can't you  instead try to convey the deep sorrow of  a magnificent beast doomed to a slow and  possible endless descent into ruin and  like just look at how they succeeded  with this in terms of design this is an  undead dragon doomed to live the rest of  its life clean to cliffs while its  brethren fly through the skies and  instead it's here rotting away if you  aggravated it tries to breathe fire but  vomits out bile and poison instead I  challenge you guys to look deeper into  every aspect of design in this game and  for example where some people just see a  rotting dragon others will see that deep  sorrow of a magnificent beast doomed to  die a slow and possibly endless descent  into ruin anyway I'll catch you guys  next time as we dive headfirst into Dark  Souls 2 there's going to be so much  content on this channel so keep your  eyes out later
 

